---
// WHAT WORKS. One row per thing a Salesforce developer has a name for.
//
// This page used to publish the 14 internal capability FAMILIES flat, which are our tier
// boundaries and not anyone else's vocabulary: `schema` appeared in five rows across two
// tables, `database` in three, `crypto` in two. A reader asking "does describe work?" found it
// under Supported and under Not-supported simultaneously, and could not answer their own
// question. It also led with a five-term glossary, which is the wrong thing to hand someone
// before they know whether they care.
//
// Every status here is still GENERATED from the classifier's own maps — the grouping changed,
// not the source of truth. The audit trail moved to /receipts.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/capabilities.json';

const title = 'What Fidelic can run — capability by capability';
const description =
  'What works, what works with a difference, and what is refused — grouped by the thing you would actually search for, and generated from the classifier itself.';

const LABEL = {
  full: 'Works',
  partial: 'Mostly works',
  none: 'Not supported',
};
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="back"><a href="/">← Home</a></p>
    <h1>What Fidelic can run</h1>

    <p>
      Your org is the real answer, and <a href="#yours">one command gives it to you</a>. This is
      the general shape: what works, and where it differs from a real org.
    </p>

    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table class="caps">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Capability</th><th>Status</th><th>Where it differs from a real org</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.groups.map((g) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <strong>{g.title}</strong>
                <span class="covers">{g.covers}</span>
              </td>
              <td><span class={`s s-${g.status}`}>{LABEL[g.status]}</span></td>
              <td>{g.summary}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="fine">
      Anything not listed is refused rather than guessed at: you get the file, the line, and the
      construct we do not support. Refusing is the point — a wrong answer you cannot see is
      worse than a gap you can plan around.
    </p>

    <h2 id="yours">The answer for your org</h2>
    <p>
      A general table cannot tell you whether <em>your</em> code runs; that depends on what your
      triggers actually do. Point the analyzer at your metadata and it grades every trigger you
      have, naming the reason for each refusal:
    </p>
    <pre><code>docker run --rm -v "$PWD/mdapi":/mdapi:ro \
  --entrypoint /sfapex fidelic/emulator coverage --report /mdapi</code></pre>
    <p class="fine">
      Nothing leaves your machine — it reads local files and makes no network calls. The report
      is plain text, built for pasting into a channel.
    </p>

    <h2>The words on this page</h2>
    <dl class="vocab">
      <dt><span class="s s-full">Works</span></dt>
      <dd>Your real Apex runs, and behaves as the org would.</dd>
      <dt><span class="s s-partial">Mostly works</span></dt>
      <dd>Your real Apex runs, but something named in the last column is modelled rather than
      really done, or one corner of it is refused. The row says which.</dd>
      <dt><span class="s s-none">Not supported</span></dt>
      <dd>Refused by name, with a reason and a file and line. Never silently wrong.</dd>
    </dl>

    <p class="fine">
      Your coverage report uses more precise words for the same ideas — EXECUTE, SIMULATED,
      UNRESOLVED — and <a href="/receipts#vocabulary">defines each one</a> alongside the numbers
      behind this page.
    </p>

    <p class="receipts-link">
      <a href="/receipts">Every number, and the command that produced it →</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .tablewrap{overflow-x:auto}
  table.caps{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:.95rem;margin:1rem 0 1.25rem}
  .caps th,.caps td{text-align:left;padding:.7rem .7rem;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d7de;vertical-align:top}
  .caps th{font-size:.78rem;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.04em;color:#57606a}
  .caps td:first-child{min-width:12rem}
  .covers{display:block;color:#57606a;font-size:.84rem;margin-top:.15rem}
  .s{display:inline-block;font-size:.78rem;font-weight:600;padding:.12rem .5rem;border-radius:999px;white-space:nowrap}
  .s-full{background:#dafbe1;color:#0a5a2a}
  .s-partial{background:#fff4d6;color:#7a5200}
  .s-none{background:#ffe9ec;color:#8b1d2c}
  .none{color:#6b7480;font-size:.88rem}
  .fine{font-size:.9rem;color:#57606a}
  .vocab dt{margin-top:.55rem}
  .vocab dd{margin:.15rem 0 .4rem 0;font-size:.93rem}
  .receipts-link{margin-top:2rem;font-size:.95rem}
  @media (max-width:640px){
    table.caps{font-size:.88rem}
    .caps td:first-child{min-width:8rem}
  }
</style>
